d then show that the gradients are incorrect.\r\n\r\n[EDITED] For context, that is how we author many built-in pytorch operators: they have .contiguous() calls when they need to assume the inputs are contiguous.","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pytorch/pytorch/issues/comments/2402527461/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pytorch/pytorch/issues/comments/2402644851","html_url":"https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/issues/137437#issuecomment-2402644851","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pytorch/pytorch/issues/137437","id":2402644851,"node_id":"IC_kwDOA-j9z86PNXNz","user":{"login":"alihassanijr","id":68103095,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY4MTAzMDk1","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/68103095?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/alihassanijr","html_url":"https://github.com/alihassanijr","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alihassanijr/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alihassanijr/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alihassanijr/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alihassanijr/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alihassanijr/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alihassanijr/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alihassanijr/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alihassanijr/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alihassanijr/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2024-10-09T15:23:03Z","updated_at":"2024-10-09T15:23:03Z","body":"I'll try and do that. But I want to clarify something first. Should both the forward and backward kernels call `.contiguous()` on input tensors, or is it possible to do that once in `setup_context` instead?\r\n\r\nI think in the old way of creating autograd functions, `setup_context` was essentially part of forward, so it looked like forward pass just makes everything contiguous, and stores the contiguous version of the backward pass, meaning backward pass wouldn't have to do it again. I'm not sure whether that's what actually happens, but I just want to make sure I'm not doing it in a wrong or inefficient way.","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pytorch/pytorch/issues/comments/2402644851/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pytorch/pytorch/issues/comments/2402671646","html_url":"https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/issues/137437#issuecomment-2402671646","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pytorch/pytorch/issues/137437","id":2402671646,"node_id":"IC_kwDOA-j9z86PNdwe","user":{"login":"zou3519","id":5652049,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU2NTIwNDk=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5652049?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/zou3519","html_url":"https://github.com/zou3519","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zou3519/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zou3519/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zou3519/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zou3519/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zou3519/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zou3519/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zou3519/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zou3519/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zou3519/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2024-10-09T15:34:36Z","updated_at":"2024-10-09T15:34:36Z","body":"> Should both the forward and backward kernels call .contiguous() on input tensors, or is it possible to do that once in setup_context instead?\r\n\r\nBoth of the kernels  (custom_op and custom_op_backward) need to call .contiguous() -- torch.compile/Inductor is able to optimize the inputs to `custom_op` and `custom_op_backward` and change their striding\r\n","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pytorch/pytorch/issues/comments/2402671646/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":1,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pytorch/pytorch/issues/comments/2402714650","html_url":"https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/issues/137437#issuecomment-2402714650","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pytorch/pytorch/issues/137437","id":2402714650,"node_id":"IC_kwDOA-j9z86PNoQa","user":{"login":"alihassanijr","id":68103095,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY4MTAzMDk1","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/68103095?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/alihassanijr","html_url":"https://github.com/alihassanijr","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alihassanijr/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alihassanijr/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alihassanijr/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alihassanijr/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alihassanijr/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alihassanijr/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alihassanijr/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alihassanijr/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alihassanijr/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2024-10-09T15:53:59Z","updated_at":"2024-10-09T15:55:29Z","body":"One more question: is there any additional logic that's supposed to exist to support automatic mixed precision? More specifically force all inputs to the op to be in lower precision without manually casting with `.to`?\r\n\r\nI.e. is there an equivalent for [custom_fwd](https://pytorch.org/docs/stable/notes/amp_examples.html#autocast-and-custom-autograd-functions) and [custom_bwd](https://pytorch.org/docs/stable/notes/amp_examples.html#autocast-and-custom-autograd-functions) for torch.library ops?\r\n\r\nOne of the custom operators I'm interested in takes in two tensors, one of which is a softmax output, and given that softmax might upcast to fp32, I typically had a `tensor.to(other_tensor.dtype)` cast somewhere, and I think it only makes sense to put that outside of the op, but doing that is just breaking training (a few iterations and we get nan loss).","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pytorch/pytorch/issues/comments/2402714650/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pytorch/pytorch/issues/comments/2402772739","html_url":"https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/issues/137437#issuecomment-2402772739","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pytorch/pytorch/issues/137437","id":2402772739,"node_id":"IC_kwDOA-j9z86PN2cD","user":{"login":"zou3519","id":5652049,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU2NTIwNDk=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5652049?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/zou3519","html_url":"https://github.com/zou3519","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zou3519/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zou3519/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zou3519/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.